            "text": "Makes a tensor of random numbers, spread evenly between min and max.\n\nEach click gives fresh numbers. Use it to test a chain with data that changes, as noise to mix into a signal, for random positions or colours, or wherever a starting point should be different every time.\n\nThe numbers are UNIFORM: every value between min and max is equally likely, so the picture has no structure at all. For randomness with a shape (a bell curve, say) see the t.dist nodes, such as t.dist.normal.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.rand <dim 0: int> <dim 1: int> ... <dtype> <device>\nOne whole number per dimension. t.rand 12 12 is a 12 by 12 tensor;\nt.rand 100 a row of 100. With no numbers you get a 1-element tensor.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nt.rand 12 12\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin (button):\nThe small button at the top left of the node. Click it, or send it anything, for a new tensor - there is no need for a separate button node. Only this inlet makes the node send.\n\nshape (text, also an inlet):\nThe dimensions, typed as numbers separated by spaces or commas. A list or tensor of numbers, or a single number, sent into the inlet works too. A new shape does not send; the next click uses it.\n\nmin / max (drag floats, also inlets):\nThe range. Setting them does NOT make a new tensor by itself - the next click uses them. In the demo each float goes through 't bang float', which passes the number to the inlet first and THEN clicks the node, so dragging it gives you new noise at once.\n\ndtype (menu):\nThe number type. float32 is the default and what most torch operations want. The menu also offers float16, int64, uint8, bool, complex64 and complex128; 'float' is the same as float32, and each name appears a second time with a 'torch.' prefix, which means the same thing. There is no 64-bit float here.\n\ndevice (menu):\nWhere the tensor is made: cpu (the default), and mps or cuda when the machine has them. Tensors on different devices cannot be combined, so keep a chain on one device.\n\nrequires_grad (checkbox):\nRecords operations for automatic differentiation. Leave it off unless you are training something. Only float and complex tensors can carry it: ticked with an integer or bool dtype, the node raises an error and sends nothing.\n\nA dtype or device name can also be typed as an argument, after the numbers - for example t.rand 12 12 int64.\nThe dtype changes what the random numbers are:\nfloat32 and float16: any value from min up to, but never exactly, max.\nint64: whole numbers from min to max, both included. Choosing int64 while max is 1 changes max to 32767.\nuint8: whole numbers from min to max, both included. Choosing it raises max to 255 and a negative min to 0, so you get the full 0 to 255.\nbool: true or false at random; min and max are ignored.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nrandom tensor:\nThe new tensor, in the shape given.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nload_bang makes the first tensor when the page opens; click the button on t.rand itself for another. The heat_map colours 0 dark purple and 1 yellow. Drag min and max towards each other and the noise flattens to a single colour; push max past 1 and cells saturate yellow. t.min and t.max report the smallest and largest value in each new tensor - always inside min and max.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.rand is the NumPy version. t.rand_like takes its shape from another tensor. t.dist.uniform does the same job as a distribution node, and t.dist.normal gives a bell curve instead. t.zeros makes a tensor of one fixed value."
        },
